1
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:13,360
Brighten up the peace,
then try to step away.

2
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:27,020
Hello, goodbye.

3
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:31,500
The one who leaves right
after the night is over.

4
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:39,840
Chuwapper!

5
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:49,360
Don't panic. In this narrow town, where are you going?
The safer way is the long way round.

6
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:58,540
If, perhaps, there's someone better than me,

7
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:03,000
tell me, tell me all night long.

8
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:12,800
I don't mind waiting.

9
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:17,020
Because I'm a Kame-za girl.

10
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:21,740
I won't lose to a Ginza girl.

11
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:26,460
I'll love you for years and years.

12
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:30,000
On the path of the Kame-za woman,

13
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:39,400
Stradi Great Pursuit!

14
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:02,120
The violin wizard said to be
the world's greatest virtuoso,

15
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,720
Mr. Ikelangelo Gandini has just arrived.

16
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:10,460
As part of his world tour, Mr. Gandini
is visiting Japan for the first time.

17
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,220
So this is Reiko's teacher

18
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:18,440
Yes, he gave me private lessons in Paris for seven years,
starting when I was five.

19
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:21,460
He's really popular, though.

20
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:25,780
I'm amazed there are so many people
in Japan who are into the violin.

21
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:29,180
Classical music has been
attracting more young fans, too.

22
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,140
To me, it just sounds like a lullaby.

23
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,830
He always smiles like this, you know,

24
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:40,600
but his lessons were strict.
Four hours every day, without fail.

25
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:44,080
That's right,
I've been invited to the party tonight too.

26
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:47,295
Is that so? Actually, I'm attending too.

27
00:02:47,345 --> 00:02:51,300
My company is supporting this visit to Japan,
you see.

28
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:54,799
It's a party limited to 100 people, right?

29
00:02:54,849 --> 00:02:58,178
Even though only 100 people
from all over Japan are invited,

30
00:02:58,228 --> 00:03:01,100
two people from our station are attending?

31
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,720
I heard only very close acquaintances were invited,
you know.

32
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:07,880
Naturally, there'll be fancy food too,

33
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:10,220
the kind of stuff you
rarely get to eat, I bet.

34
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:14,760
Alright! Take me along too!

35
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:18,700
Come on, one more person won't matter, you
can sneak me in without an invitation, right?

36
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:20,940
Well, I guess I could,

37
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,720
but senpai,
you have to come in a suit and tie.

38
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:25,540
And you can't make a scene, got it?

39
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:28,500
I know that. Don't treat me like a child!

40
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:49,920
Bravo!

41
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:55,220
That moved me so much I'm near tears.

42
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:57,440
What a wonderful performance, Ryo-chan.

43
00:03:58,660 --> 00:03:59,340
Yeah, it was.

44
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,320
That famous Stradivarius

45
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,280
has to be mastered like your own body.

46
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:07,060
Otherwise, you won't get that sound.

47
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:10,520
Eat too much and your belly sticks out.

48
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,260
What are you talking about?

49
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:13,800
It's a Stradivarius.

50
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,160
The violin your teacher owns.

51
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:19,460
Is that Strad-whatever
violin really that amazing?

52
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:21,580
It's a Stradivarius.

53
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:23,640
It's the most famous
instrument in the world.

54
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:26,980
If we're talking about
Mr. Gandini's Stradivarius,

55
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:29,660
it's probably worth at least 300 million yen,
don't you think?

56
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,420
You see, Ryo-chan.

57
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,080
Is this Stradivarius
really worth 300 million?

58
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:37,040
It's a Stradivarius.

59
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,940
From the 17th to the 18th century,

60
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,000
in the Cremona region of Italy,

61
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,240
made by Antonio Stradivari,

62
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,740
it's the greatest violin in history.

63
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:49,420
Antonio? All I know is Inoki.

64
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:53,720
People who aren't into violins
don't know much about it.

65
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:56,220
But no matter how good the instrument is,

66
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:59,000
without the skill to play it perfectly,

67
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:01,080
it's a waste, you know.

68
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:05,280
He's truly the best virtuoso in the world.

69
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:09,440
He's a different kind of person from us,
isn't he?

70
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:14,220
Excuse me for a moment.

71
00:05:15,060 --> 00:05:15,940
Reiko-kun.

72
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:17,720
Professor Gandini!

73
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:21,100
Oh my, you've grown so big!

74
00:05:22,420 --> 00:05:24,580
Sensei, please put me down.

75
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:27,240
I got a little carried away.

76
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:31,160
When I was teaching you,
you were still this small.

77
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:33,820
There was none, was there.

78
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:37,120
What a larger-than-life teacher.

79
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:39,760
Is this your friend?

80
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,320
I'm Nakagawa.

81
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,620
My father often talks about you, Sensei,

82
00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:46,780
Mister Ryuichiro's

83
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:48,360
Pleased to meet you.

84
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:50,340
And this is

85
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:51,380
my senior.

86
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:53,460
Senpai, nice to meet you.

87
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:54,960
No.

88
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:58,440
My name isn't "Senpai," you know.

89
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:00,720
Well, never mind.

90
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:04,380
It hurts...

91
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:06,520
Senpai, you're eating too much.

92
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:08,460
Hey, Ryo-chan, Kei-chan.

93
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,220
I'm in a bit of a fix. Professor Gandini...

94
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:13,980
he says he wants to
see Japan's old downtown.

95
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:15,460
Old downtown?

96
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:17,920
But his schedule is packed, isn't it?

97
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:20,860
It is. What should I do?

98
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:25,680
I thought we lived in
completely different worlds.

99
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:27,840
Maybe he's easier to talk to than I thought.

100
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:30,440
Alright, I'll let them go.

101
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:31,900
Let them go?

102
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:31,960
Seriously?

103
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:34,080
You touched it! You touched it!

104
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:34,960
Don't panic.

105
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:41,040
That's strange,
Professor Gandini isn't here.

106
00:06:42,980 --> 00:06:44,620
What should we do, senpai?

107
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:47,980
Never mind that,
let's go straight for the elevator!

108
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:48,800
Yes.

109
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:51,740
Now! Let's go.

110
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:55,500
Senpai.

111
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:57,480
Nakagawa, quick, the door!

112
00:06:57,480 --> 00:06:57,820
Yes.

113
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:03,920
Good, that worked!

114
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:06,020
But what do we do now?

115
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:08,660
If he wants to see old downtown,
it's Asakusa.

116
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:39,600
What? Professor Gandini is missing?

117
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:42,400
K-keep your voice down, President!

118
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:46,500
Manager, didn't you hear anything?

119
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:49,940
By the time I noticed, he was already gone.

120
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:52,360
And he hasn't returned
to his hotel room either.

121
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:55,800
This is terrible!
Hurry, search all of Tokyo!

122
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:58,020
If something has happened to him,
we're in big trouble.

123
00:07:58,540 --> 00:07:58,900
Yes.

124
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:30,880
Ahahahaha.

125
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:33,680
It's kind of scary how
well it gets through to me.

126
00:08:35,580 --> 00:08:37,040
Is Gin-san here?

127
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,780
It's just the old man.
The Janken Three Brothers, that comic band?

128
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:44,360
Do you folks play music too?

129
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:47,120
Everyone,
come over here and let's drink together!

130
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:49,460
Nice, Kai-jii-san.

131
00:08:49,460 --> 00:08:51,100
Pardon the intrusion, Gin-san.

132
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:53,720
Sure, come on in, come on in!

133
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:54,800
Here, trash.

134
00:08:56,260 --> 00:08:58,260
They don't know the meaning of restraint.

135
00:08:58,260 --> 00:09:01,660
Raaan, the eldest brother, Goose.

136
00:09:01,660 --> 00:09:06,180
Gegu-gu-in, loves sleeping, non, kimono.

137
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:13,080
The second brother, Chokkiro,
loves chokkichokkichokkin!

138
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:14,140
Jagarimono.

139
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:22,200
The third brother, Parr-kicchi-pappa-rappa,
a cheerful go-getter.

140
00:09:24,120 --> 00:09:28,880
All three together:
the Janken Three Brothers!

141
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:33,880
Bravo, bravo!

142
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:36,540
The professor is so happy.

143
00:09:36,540 --> 00:09:38,400
He's just like a child, isn't he?

144
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:11,880
It's fun!

145
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:13,960
Yosh!

146
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:15,560
I'm stuffed over here.

147
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,680
Pan-pakapaaan!

148
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:18,600
Please, you lunatic.

149
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:21,700
The world's greatest violin virtuoso

150
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:25,060
gives it to Drake.

151
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:28,720
Well, there's no helping this now.

152
00:10:29,640 --> 00:10:32,780
Fitting in with the downtown
atmosphere this well!

153
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:47,640
Is it morning already?

154
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:51,480
Oh no, I have work in Osaka today!

155
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:53,880
Hey, wake up, Gusuke, chokiro!

156
00:10:57,420 --> 00:10:58,480
I'm still sleepy.

157
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:02,440
Wake up! If you're late,
you'll never get another call.

158
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:09,740
Hey, we won't make it! Hurry, hurry!

159
00:11:15,220 --> 00:11:18,460
Reiko-kun, last night was fun, wasn't it?

160
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:22,680
Yes, but Professor,
is today's concert all right?

161
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:25,760
Don't worry.

162
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:29,240
What's wrong?

163
00:11:29,240 --> 00:11:32,460
No, this isn't my violin!

164
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:49,300
What? You're saying it's not
the 300-million-yen Stradivarius?

165
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:52,080
It's a Stradivarius, senpai.

166
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:53,920
Got it, got it.

167
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:57,320
Is it really different? How is it different?

168
00:11:58,460 --> 00:12:01,500
I know a Stradivarius like my own body.

169
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:12,540
I can tell at a glance. But if this one's wrong,
then Parki-san from the Janken Three Brothers must have mistakenly taken the Stradivarius.

170
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:14,900
This is terrible, we have to do something.

171
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:19,060
If I show this off to Dad...

172
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:26,060
I can't drink, and I'm not talking about sake.
I don't know where the Ruchō Supā-tachi went, though.

173
00:12:26,440 --> 00:12:27,767
Uhh,

174
00:12:27,817 --> 00:12:31,260
Didn't he say he had work in Osaka today?

175
00:12:31,260 --> 00:12:32,760
Osaka!?

176
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:34,240
We have to get it back quickly.

177
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:36,040
The concert starts at four.

178
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:37,360
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

179
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:40,480
Don't worry! I'll definitely get it back!

180
00:12:40,820 --> 00:12:41,400
Let's go!

181
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:42,020
Yes!

182
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:56,920
Damn, we were too late!

183
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:00,340
What do we do now, Ryo-chan?

184
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:03,440
Nakagawa, call a helicopter!

185
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:03,960
Yes.

186
00:13:07,880 --> 00:13:09,460
Leave it to you, senpai.

187
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:10,900
Ryo-chan, I'm counting on you.

188
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:15,020
We have to chase the
bullet train right away!

189
00:13:15,020 --> 00:13:15,660
Yes.

190
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:29,400
Then we'll head to the
venue with Professor Gandini.

191
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:30,000
Right.

192
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:34,640
Alright, there it is!

193
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:35,900
Let's go!

194
00:13:35,900 --> 00:13:36,420
Yes.

195
00:13:40,180 --> 00:13:43,160
Slowly! Slowly! Lower it slowly!

196
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:48,400
Damn, the riverbank is in the way!

197
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:56,500
Yes!

198
00:13:57,100 --> 00:13:58,320
We did it!

199
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:11,580
Ngh! It fell, Maru-kun!

200
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:16,220
Ow!

201
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:19,080
Hey! Goo, Jojipan! Wake up!

202
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:24,160
Shit! I didn't notice 'cause
I was too comfy sleeping!

203
00:14:28,940 --> 00:14:31,360
Hey! It's over! Bakyū!

204
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:40,600
We were so close!

205
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:49,700
Phew, I'm so relieved.

206
00:14:50,180 --> 00:14:52,280
Thank goodness the professor came back.

207
00:14:52,760 --> 00:14:54,760
If he's gone to see his former student,

208
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:57,020
he should have just said so.

209
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:00,820
Before the concert, the professor

210
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:03,740
is trying to build up his focus like this.

211
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:06,380
That's right, please keep it quiet.

212
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:10,620
Yes, well, we'll be over there.

213
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:20,080
We don't have much time left, do we?

214
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:22,120
I wonder if Ryo-chan will make it in time.

215
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:32,360
Aaaaaaah!

216
00:15:36,250 --> 00:15:38,240
Agisolivity!

217
00:15:39,470 --> 00:15:40,240
Choina!

218
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:51,820
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah!

219
00:15:52,210 --> 00:15:53,580
Noooooo!

220
00:15:56,040 --> 00:15:57,220
Aaaaaaah!

221
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:01,720
Aaaaaaah!

222
00:16:07,220 --> 00:16:10,660
The crowd's really into it today!
Let's go even higher next time!

223
00:16:10,660 --> 00:16:12,180
Alright, leave it to me!

224
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:13,400
Aaaaaaah!

225
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:16,320
That's a Stradivarius!

226
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:17,520
Aaaaaaah!

227
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:19,540
What're you doing?

228
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:24,320
Got a complaint? Speak up quietly, then.

229
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:26,580
It's nothing.

230
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:30,280
Until we meet again, on that day.

231
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:33,000
Janken Three Brothers

232
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:44,400
What's wrong?

233
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:46,860
The sound's off somehow, you know.

234
00:16:46,860 --> 00:16:48,480
Isn't it broken?

235
00:16:49,620 --> 00:16:52,140
I'm sure there's a workshop near here.

236
00:16:52,140 --> 00:16:53,880
Want to try holding it?

237
00:16:58,380 --> 00:16:59,900
Which way did they go?

238
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:07,060
Wow, this is amazing.

239
00:17:07,660 --> 00:17:10,040
The place has been packed
for over an hour already.

240
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:11,940
Well, of course.

241
00:17:12,460 --> 00:17:16,440
We won't be able to hear the professor play live for a long time,
you know.

242
00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:21,480
I wonder what Ryo-chan is doing.

243
00:17:22,040 --> 00:17:24,720
Waiting like this just makes me restless,
doesn't it?

244
00:17:28,040 --> 00:17:29,680
Still, he's really something.

245
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:32,960
Being so composed even at a time like this.

246
00:17:36,380 --> 00:17:39,620
Damn it, where the hell did those guys go?

247
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:46,580
There he is!

248
00:17:47,420 --> 00:17:48,900
Hey! Rock-Scissors-Paper!

249
00:17:49,960 --> 00:17:51,080
Another bookmark again?

250
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,700
We've been getting nothing but
those since the start of the month.

251
00:17:55,220 --> 00:17:56,200
Hey, take a look, Haru.

252
00:17:56,200 --> 00:17:58,040
Look at the number of these violins...

253
00:17:59,560 --> 00:18:03,320
With Gandini's visit to Japan,
the violin boom is great, but...

254
00:18:03,880 --> 00:18:07,960
With this many of them,
it'll take a year to finish.

255
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:10,760
Anyway, that's what the boss says.

256
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:13,480
Don't say that, just take a quick look.

257
00:18:13,900 --> 00:18:14,840
It never gets old.

258
00:18:16,440 --> 00:18:19,720
I don't really know. The boss is out too.

259
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:24,025
I see. But it sounds different from usual.

260
00:18:24,075 --> 00:18:26,040
Maybe it's the humidity.

261
00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:28,600
Finally caught up.

262
00:18:29,100 --> 00:18:30,940
Why? It's Kankichi!

263
00:18:31,300 --> 00:18:32,900
You guys, that violin...

264
00:18:35,240 --> 00:18:36,320
It's a madman!

265
00:18:40,980 --> 00:18:43,080
I can't tell which one is which anymore!

266
00:18:43,860 --> 00:18:47,240
What's wrong, Motokichi?
Why are you so flustered?

267
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:51,200
What do you mean what's wrong?
It's 300 million yen! 300 million yen!

268
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:53,320
What about the 300 million yen?

269
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:56,360
You two grabbed the wrong violin.

270
00:18:56,360 --> 00:18:57,920
This is the one you took.

271
00:18:57,920 --> 00:19:01,780
That violin is a Stradivarius
worth 300 million yen!

272
00:19:02,260 --> 00:19:04,600
What? A Stradivarius?

273
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:06,740
So which one is it?

274
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:08,920
Doesn't anyone know which is which?

275
00:19:08,920 --> 00:19:10,180
It's impossible, I tell you.

276
00:19:11,020 --> 00:19:14,000
At a glance,
they look no different from our violins.

277
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:15,780
How would anyone possibly know?

278
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:17,140
You should be able to tell by the sound.

279
00:19:17,140 --> 00:19:19,680
Even if we listen, we can't tell, Kankichi.

280
00:19:21,140 --> 00:19:23,280
What do we do? We don't have any time left!

281
00:19:32,120 --> 00:19:33,760
The performance is about to start.

282
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:35,280
Professor.

283
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:38,820
Professor.

284
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:40,560
Professor, it's almost time.

285
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:44,720
Well, the thing is,
it's a bit of a problem...

286
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:45,980
The professor, he's...

287
00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:47,660
Professor.

288
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:49,920
Were you asleep?

289
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:54,060
Reiko, isn't it about
time for my rice porridge?

290
00:19:54,620 --> 00:19:56,420
But the Stradivarius...

291
00:19:58,120 --> 00:19:58,920
Yes.

292
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:00,800
Yes, senior.

293
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:06,900
We got the Stradivarius back.

294
00:20:07,360 --> 00:20:07,640
Yes.

295
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:09,520
Anyway, come outside.

296
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:11,140
Outside?

297
00:20:12,500 --> 00:20:14,320
Senior Hasu says to go
outside and take a look.

298
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:15,440
Outside?

299
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:20,400
Where's the Stradivarius?

300
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:22,640
Your teacher's Stradivarius

301
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:24,560
please take a look.

302
00:20:30,120 --> 00:20:31,640
Nakagawa, Reiko.

303
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:34,460
It should be here somewhere.

304
00:20:39,440 --> 00:20:43,220
Which one is the Stradivarius,
which one is the Stradivarius?

305
00:20:43,900 --> 00:20:49,500
Are we really searching through all of these?
If we check every single one, it'll take forever.

306
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:53,600
It couldn't be helped.
There's nothing I could do about it.

307
00:21:01,800 --> 00:21:02,200
Maestro.

308
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:05,500
Everyone is waiting for my performance.

309
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:07,160
But from among these,

310
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:12,020
Hey, hey.

311
00:21:12,020 --> 00:21:14,200
Even if amateurs can't tell the difference,

312
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:16,420
can you really just pick them so randomly?

313
00:21:18,380 --> 00:21:19,180
Hmph.

314
00:21:26,440 --> 00:21:28,460
We apologize for keeping
you waiting so long.

315
00:21:29,320 --> 00:21:31,780
We will now begin the violin wizard's

316
00:21:31,780 --> 00:21:34,040
violin solo concert by Michelangelo Gandini.

317
00:21:34,040 --> 00:21:37,220
The concert is now beginning.

318
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:50,820
Something picked that carelessly
can't possibly be a Stradivarius.

319
00:21:51,240 --> 00:21:52,820
We're finished.

320
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:02,000
This one?

321
00:22:04,680 --> 00:22:06,240
It's a Stradivarius, you know.

322
00:22:07,220 --> 00:22:08,080
You guys.

323
00:22:08,080 --> 00:22:10,980
No doubt about it.

324
00:22:52,720 --> 00:22:54,860
Sensei, that was a wonderful performance.

325
00:22:54,860 --> 00:22:59,220
We were truly treated to the sound
of the world's finest Stradivarius.

326
00:22:59,220 --> 00:23:02,780
But I just picked that violin at random,
you know.

327
00:23:02,780 --> 00:23:04,400
You figured it out

328
00:23:04,400 --> 00:23:06,100
It was pure coincidence, pure coincidence.

329
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:11,620
Sensei knew it at a single glance.

330
00:23:11,620 --> 00:23:16,520
After all, the Stradivarius has
become a part of Sensei's body.

331
00:23:26,780 --> 00:23:29,220
Reiko-kun, Mr. Nakagawa, senpai,

332
00:23:29,270 --> 00:23:31,760
thank you for everything.

333
00:23:32,540 --> 00:23:34,560
I'll come back next year. Ciao.

334
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:35,420
See you.

335
00:23:37,640 --> 00:23:39,834
Senpai, girls' tsukudani,

336
00:23:39,884 --> 00:23:41,880
I'm looking forward to it.

337
00:23:43,720 --> 00:23:47,580
It's not "girls," it's
"shijoshi," I told you. Take care.

338
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:14,580
It's the feeling, it's the feeling,
the thing I want to give you

339
00:24:14,580 --> 00:24:22,700
It's the feeling,
it's the feeling, it's my feeling

340
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:30,260
Once you shoulder the heavy load,

341
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:38,100
your hands will be free.

342
00:24:38,100 --> 00:24:46,000
If those hands could support someone,

343
00:24:46,660 --> 00:24:53,900
how wonderful that would be.

344
00:24:54,500 --> 00:24:59,200
It's the feeling, it's the feeling,

345
00:25:00,260 --> 00:25:01,520
the thing I want to give

346
00:25:03,060 --> 00:25:05,020
is the feeling.

347
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:07,000
It's the feeling.

348
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,620
It's my feeling.

349
00:25:18,780 --> 00:25:23,660
Hey! Who the hell are you?

350
00:25:23,920 --> 00:25:25,680
Why do you look exactly like me?

351
00:25:25,960 --> 00:25:28,040
Why are you so fast?

352
00:25:28,040 --> 00:25:31,040
And why are there so many of you?

353
00:25:31,540 --> 00:25:36,500
Next time: SF Alien Counterattack...
what's that about? Tell me!

